{
  "@context": {
    "schema": "https://schema.org/"
  },
  "@type": "Note",
  "@id": "note:notion-127",
  "title": "Technical Requirements (Deep Research)",
  "author": "person:jane-brew:001",
  "status": "draft",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "b-1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Technical Requirements (Deep Research)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-2",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The following is findings from research into the key areas of technical, workflow, user, content management, and legal requirements for Docket TV (a broadcast management app for Cablecast users). It includes best practices for database structures, video format support, scheduling complexities, integration needs, user roles, compliance concerns, and more."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-13",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Database Structure:** Opt for a relational database schema to handle complex relationships between media assets, schedules, and metadata. Relational DBMS (e.g. SQL Server, MySQL, Oracle) are standard in broadcast systems because they scale and allow efficient querying of linked data ([Database design for broadcasters | TV Tech](https://www.tvtechnology.com/miscellaneous/database-design-for-broadcasters#:~:text=There%20are%20many%20types%20of,enough%20for%20complex%20data%20types)). Design the schema with separate tables for **Content Assets** (individual media files) and **Metadata Records** (e.g. a show or series record) and link them via unique IDs. This supports one-to-many relations like series-to-episodes and content-to-schedule entries ([Database design for broadcasters | TV Tech](https://www.tvtechnology.com/miscellaneous/database-design-for-broadcasters#:~:text=attributes,of%20Air%20Force%20One)). For example, a “series” entry can link to multiple episode assets, and a schedule entry links to a content ID and a timeslot. Ensure the schema captures attributes needed by different departments (e.g. rights, contracts, technical specs) but remains extensible for new fields ([Database design for broadcasters | TV Tech](https://www.tvtechnology.com/miscellaneous/database-design-for-broadcasters#:~:text=The%20first%20step%20is%20to,might%20miss%20initially%20are%20very))."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Video File Formats & Codecs:** Support a wide range of broadcast-friendly file formats and codecs to accommodate various sources. Cable broadcasters often use MPEG-2 for legacy systems and H.264 (MPEG-4 AVC) for HD content. Professional mezzanine codecs like Apple ProRes or Avid DNxHD are also common for high-quality content exchange (). The system should handle wrappers like **MOV/MP4** (for H.264, ProRes, DNxHD, DV formats), **MPEG-PS/TS** files (for MPEG-2 video with MPEG-1 Layer II audio), and possibly **AVI** or **MXF** if required ([support.cablecast.tv](https://support.cablecast.tv/s/articles/cablecast-supported-file-formats#:~:text=MOV%20%2F%20MP4%20files)) ([support.cablecast.tv](https://support.cablecast.tv/s/articles/cablecast-supported-file-formats#:~:text=MPEG%20files)). Ensure the chosen codecs meet quality needs and are compatible with the playout servers – e.g. Cablecast servers can play files in MPEG-2, H.264, DV/DVCPro, ProRes, etc., without transcoding (). Standardize on a codec profile for in-house encoding (H.264 is widely supported for HD, while MPEG-2 might be used for SD or older systems) and ensure audio meets required specs (48 kHz audio, proper channel configuration) ([support.cablecast.tv](https://support.cablecast.tv/s/articles/cablecast-supported-file-formats#:~:text=PCM%20audio%20restrictions)). Include support for closed caption data in the media files (e.g. CEA-608/708 or SCTE-128 in H.264 streams) so that captions pass through to broadcast ([support.cablecast.tv](https://support.cablecast.tv/s/articles/cablecast-supported-file-formats#:~:text=Closed%20captioning%20and%20AFD))."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-5",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Time Zone Handling:** Implement a clear strategy for time zones so scheduling is accurate across regions. **Use local event times with timezone metadata** rather than converting to UTC for future broadcasts – this avoids errors around daylight savings changes ([datetime - How should I handle timezones in this context? - Stack Overflow](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/78024890/how-should-i-handle-timezones-in-this-context#:~:text=I%20linked%20to%20several%20different,event%27s%20local%20time%20zone%20identifier)). Each channel or station can have a designated default time zone, and schedule entries are stored with the local date/time and a time zone identifier. The system should convert and display times appropriately for remote users or aggregated views. For example, a program set to air at 8:00 PM EST will automatically show as 5:00 PM PST for a user in California. Be mindful of daylight saving transitions – an event scheduled at “2:00 AM” in a region with DST might be ambiguous once a year, so include logic to handle those exceptions (possibly by storing an absolute UTC timestamp internally alongside the local time for unambiguous reference). Clearly communicate the time zone in the UI and any exported schedules to avoid confusion."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-6",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Bandwidth & Storage:** Plan for significant storage and network bandwidth to handle large video files and continuous playout. High-definition video files can be **several GB per hour** (e.g. ~4 GB/hour for H.264 at broadcast quality or >20 GB/hour for intra-frame codecs around 100 Mbps). A single channel playout server may need to sustain 10–20 Mbps per stream for HD content, so ensure the storage system and network can deliver that data rate *per channel* concurrently. For example, Cablecast’s video servers include up to 10–20 TB of RAID storage and can decode ~220 Mbps total, supporting multiple HD streams smoothly (). Estimate storage based on content volume: e.g. 1 hour of 1080i MPEG-2 @ 25 Mbps is ~11 GB, whereas 1 hour of 1080p H.264 @ 8 Mbps is ~3.6 GB. If the station archives months of programming, this quickly requires tens of terabytes. Therefore, use high-throughput shared storage (NAS/SAN or local RAID arrays) and possibly tiered storage (moving older content to cheaper storage). For **bandwidth**, consider both **playout bandwidth** (streaming from storage to channel outputs) and **transfer bandwidth** for content ingest or delivery (uploading new programs, sending files to VOD platforms, etc.). Gigabit Ethernet (or 10GbE for multiple HD channels) is recommended for internal networks to prevent any lag in content delivery. If content is distributed to OTT or streaming platforms, account for transcoding and uplink bandwidth as well. Plan headroom for peak usage, such as multiple channels airing high-bitrate content simultaneously or copying new files while playout is ongoing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-7",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**APIs & Integration:** Use robust APIs to integrate the scheduling system with existing broadcast infrastructure. The system should expose and consume APIs for key functions: for instance, an API to **export schedules** or EPG data to listings services or websites ([Broadcast Management System and TV Scheduling Software | VSN](https://www.vsn-tv.com/en/products/vsncrea/#:~:text=programming%20is%20scheduled%20using%20virtual,file%20system%20or%20via%20API)), and APIs to **control or update playout automation** systems (triggering the playout server, switching live inputs, etc.). Integration with third-party systems is crucial – e.g. an interface to a **broadcast automation server** for sending the daily playlist, an API to a **content management/MAM** system to pull media metadata, and hooks to an **ERP or traffic system** for advertising insertion and billing ([Broadcast Management System and TV Scheduling Software | VSN](https://www.vsn-tv.com/en/products/vsncrea/#:~:text=VSNCrea%20easily%20integrates%20with%20third,End%20solutions)). Ensure the system can ingest data from external sources too (e.g. importing program listings, or receiving as-run logs from automation). Modern broadcast management solutions offer RESTful APIs for most actions, so external apps or websites can query what’s “On Air” now or upcoming programs. For example, the system can use an API to push “now/next” info to a channel’s on-air graphics or digital signage () (). Additionally, plan for **Emergency Alert System (EAS)** integration – typically via GPIO triggers or network commands from EAS equipment – so that when an emergency alert is received, the automation can be interrupted (see *Legal & Compliance*). All API integrations should be well-documented and secure, using authentication, since they may interface with critical control systems."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-8",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Content Approval Process:** Define a clear workflow from content creation to broadcast approval. Typically, a producer or contributor submits a program (or a file is ingested into the system) which then enters a review queue. A **content manager or program director reviews** the media for technical quality and compliance (checking that video/audio meet standards and no disallowed content) and approves it for scheduling. The system should support status tags like “Draft/Pending,” “Under Review,” “Approved for Air,” etc., with notifications at each step. Collaboration tools (comments, version review) are helpful – for example, multiple team members might add notes or timestamped comments on a preview, and the approver can sign off once required edits are made ([MAM: Organize and Manage Media Content](https://www.orangelogic.com/media-asset-management-mam-in-digital-asset-management#:~:text=6,edit%2C%20or%20distribute%20media%20assets)). Once approved, the content becomes available in the scheduling library for the schedulers. It’s best practice to maintain an **electronic trail of approvals** – i.e., log who approved what and when – for accountability. In some operations, there may be multiple approval layers (e.g. an **editorial approval** for content and a **technical approval** for quality). The scheduling system should be flexible to accommodate these steps, possibly with simple state transitions or checkboxes. By enforcing an approval step, accidental broadcasting of unreviewed content is prevented."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-9",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Managing Schedule Conflicts & Interruptions:** Provide tools to avoid and resolve schedule conflicts. The system should not allow two programs to be scheduled on the same channel at overlapping times unless explicitly intended (like a dual-feed). Implement conflict checking that immediately flags overlaps or gaps. If a conflict occurs (say two producers scheduled different content for the same slot), the scheduler should be alerted and able to easily move or bump one item. Many broadcasters schedule content sequentially, so a best practice is to have the system auto-calculate end times based on durations to prevent overlap. For **last-minute changes or emergency interruptions**, the workflow must be agile: allow inserting a higher-priority event that preempts the scheduled programming. For example, if there’s a breaking emergency or a live event that runs long, users should be able to **trigger an override** – possibly by dragging an “Emergency Alert” placeholder into the schedule or activating a live input. The system should then either push the scheduled programs to a later slot or mark them as interrupted. It’s crucial that all departments see these changes in real-time ([Broadcast Management System and TV Scheduling Software | VSN](https://www.vsn-tv.com/en/products/vsncrea/#:~:text=Maximum%20synchronization)). A “delay” or “kill” option for scheduled items helps manage unscheduled extensions or cancellations. After an interruption, workflows may include deciding how to reschedule the missed content or fill the remaining time. Ensure the system logs any deviations (for compliance logs) and, if possible, automates the recovery – e.g., resumes the playlist where it left off, or switches to a standby content if a live source fails. Clear procedures (and possibly on-screen prompts) for emergency scenarios are a must so that even under pressure, staff can execute the interruption workflow quickly and correctly."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-10",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Metadata for Categorizing & Search:** Rich metadata is essential for organizing content and powering search within the system. Each content item should carry descriptive metadata such as **title, synopsis, genre/category, keywords, production date, content rating, language, and contributor/producer name**. This allows schedulers and librarians to quickly find content by various criteria (e.g. find all “children’s education” programs under 30 minutes). **Categorization** can be multi-level: e.g. tags for genre (News, Talk Show, Sports), flags for special interests (election coverage, COVID-19, etc.), and series/episode info for episodic content. A well-structured metadata schema improves findability – the system might let users define custom fields or metadata templates per content type ([MAM: Organize and Manage Media Content](https://www.orangelogic.com/media-asset-management-mam-in-digital-asset-management#:~:text=Key%20Components%20of%20MAM)) ([Broadcast Management System and TV Scheduling Software | VSN](https://www.vsn-tv.com/en/products/vsncrea/#:~:text=VSNCrea%20can%20be%20easily%20customized,the%20choice%20of%20the%20customer)). For example, a **series episode** entry should include series name, season and episode number, while a one-time event might include an event date. All this information should be indexed for search, so users can query “producer = John Doe” or “keyword = water quality” to retrieve relevant programs. **Best practice:** follow an industry metadata standard if available (e.g. TV-Anytime or Dublin Core elements) to ensure consistency ([Standards: Part 26 - An Introduction To Metadata - The Broadcast Bridge - Connecting IT to Broadcast](https://www.thebroadcastbridge.com/content/entry/20984/standards-part-26-an-introduction-to-metadata#:~:text=Anything%20else%20that%20is%20not,the%20playback%20process%20is%20Metadata)) ([Standards: Part 26 - An Introduction To Metadata - The Broadcast Bridge - Connecting IT to Broadcast](https://www.thebroadcastbridge.com/content/entry/20984/standards-part-26-an-introduction-to-metadata#:~:text=Metadata%20helps%20to%20make%20the,with%20the%20essence%20format%20design)). This also helps when exporting program listings to external systems or guides. In summary, define a core set of metadata fields that are required (to ensure nothing important is omitted) and provide advanced search and filter tools so that users can easily locate content in a large library."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-11",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Advance Planning Horizon:** Determine how far ahead schedules are created, and ensure the system supports both long-term planning and last-minute changes. Many broadcasters plan key programming **weeks or months in advance**. For instance, major networks might sketch out a programming grid a year ahead for prime-time slots (especially for known series or events), and typically lock the detailed schedule at least 1–2 weeks before air (to publish listings) ([How far ahead are TV schedules planned? — Digital Spy](https://forums.digitalspy.com/discussion/558404/how-far-ahead-are-tv-schedules-planned#:~:text=Planning%20can%20be%20upto%20three,the%20listings%20magazines%20come%20out)). Community or cable access channels might work on a shorter horizon (perhaps scheduling one month at a time, with weekly adjustments). The system should allow scheduling entries well into the future (at least 6–12 months ahead) and also handle recurring placeholders (e.g. a daily news show at 6 PM every weekday). **Long-term repeats:** If a show has a consistent slot, the software could offer a repeat/series scheduling feature (schedule every Monday 8 PM for the next 3 months). Best practice is to maintain a **rolling schedule** – for example, always have the next 2-3 weeks finalized, the next 2-3 months in draft form. The software should also facilitate publishing the schedule for viewer guides or listings once it’s finalized (often deadlines ~1-2 weeks before air for TV guides). And when those last-minute adjustments happen (they inevitably do), the system should track changes. The goal is to balance having a solid plan (so content and promos can be prepared) with flexibility for unforeseen changes. A good system might even allow versioning of a schedule or comparison between the original plan and final as-run schedule to analyze how much changed last minute."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-12",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Reporting & Analytics:** Broadcasters need reports on both operations and content metrics. The system should provide **as-run logs** that detail what actually aired, including time, content ID, and duration – this is vital for compliance and for reporting to any stakeholders (e.g. a sponsor or regulator) ([Broadcast Management System and TV Scheduling Software | VSN](https://www.vsn-tv.com/en/products/vsncrea/#:~:text=Maximum%20synchronization)). Additionally, analytics can help improve programming decisions. Key reports include:"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-14",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Schedule adherence and changes:** report of any missed or shifted programs, showing schedule vs. actual."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-15",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Content usage:** how many times each program ran, total hours of each category (e.g. 40% news, 30% local talk shows, 30% bulletin board). This can help meet any content quotas or simply analyze programming mix."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-16",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Advance schedule planning:** a calendar view or listing that can be exported for publication (weekly/monthly schedule for a channel)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-17",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Metadata reports:** e.g. list all content set to expire soon, or all content without a rating or without captions (to catch issues)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-18",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Producer activity:** how many programs each producer contributed, etc., if needed for community media."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-19",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Technical reports:** storage usage by category, number of files, etc., which can aid in capacity planning."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-20",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Audience and engagement analytics:** If available, integrate any ratings or viewership data. For instance, if the system can get **ratings data** (from Nielsen or a web analytics for streaming platforms), it should correlate it with the schedule. VSN’s system, for example, integrates with rating services like IBOPE via API to tie audience metrics to the schedule ([Broadcast Management System and TV Scheduling Software | VSN](https://www.vsn-tv.com/en/products/vsncrea/#:~:text=VSNCrea%20easily%20integrates%20with%20third,End%20solutions)). For Cablecast, which often serves community channels, direct Nielsen ratings may not exist, but online platform analytics (like VOD views or live stream viewer counts) could be reported."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-21",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Advertising/PSA reports:** if the channel runs ads or public service announcements, reports on spots aired, counts, and fulfillment of ad schedules are needed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-22",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The system should allow custom report generation (with filters by date range, channel, content type, etc.) and export to common formats (CSV, PDF). Ideally, analytics dashboards are built-in, so a user can quickly see key metrics (perhaps a summary of how many hours of new content aired this month vs repeats, etc.). In short, robust reporting and analytics help broadcasters demonstrate compliance, measure performance, and optimize scheduling decisions ([Broadcast Management System and TV Scheduling Software | VSN](https://www.vsn-tv.com/en/products/vsncrea/#:~:text=Maximum%20synchronization)) ([Broadcast Management System and TV Scheduling Software | VSN](https://www.vsn-tv.com/en/products/vsncrea/#:~:text=VSNCrea%20easily%20integrates%20with%20third,End%20solutions))."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-24",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-23",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Key User Roles & Responsibilities:** Identify the primary user roles and tailor the system to their needs. Common roles in a broadcast scheduling environment include:"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-25",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Scheduler/Programmer:** Responsible for assembling the daily/weekly schedule. They use the content library to place programs in timeslots, resolve conflicts, and ensure the lineup meets the programming strategy. They need an intuitive calendar interface and tools to adjust the schedule quickly."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-26",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Content Manager/Librarian:** Manages the media library and metadata. They ingest new content (or oversee automated ingestion), enter or verify metadata, and perform quality control. They may also handle content tagging, categorization, and rights management. This role ensures that only approved, well-described content is available for scheduling."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-27",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Producer/Contributor:** In community media, producers (e.g. independent content creators or staff producers) might upload their shows and provide initial metadata. They might not have scheduling power but can see when their content is slated to air. Their focus is on content submission and making sure all required information (title, descriptions, etc.) is provided."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-28",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Administrator/Station Manager:** Oversees the entire system and workflow. This role can manage user accounts, permissions, system settings, and has ultimate control to publish or adjust schedules. They often also need high-level reports (for instance, to report the channel’s activities to management or a board)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-29",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Other possible roles include **Ad Sales/Traffic Coordinator** (if the channel sells advertising, this person would schedule commercials and ensure they air as contracted) and **Technical Operator** (monitoring the automation playback and resolving technical issues, possibly using the system to manually trigger events or monitor feed status)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-30",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Each role should have a clear interface or dashboard in the system: e.g. producers see their submissions and their status, schedulers see the channel grid and content pool, etc."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-35",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-31",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Permission Levels:** Implement role-based access control so each user only accesses what they need. For example, **Producers** might have permission to create and edit their own content records, but not to schedule shows or delete other content. **Schedulers** can modify the schedule and maybe edit metadata, but perhaps cannot permanently delete content or change system settings. **Content managers** have rights to approve content, edit any metadata, and manage the library (adding/removing content). **Admins** have full permissions, including configuring channels, overriding schedules, and user management. The system should allow setting these permission levels and possibly more granular rights (e.g. a user can be given “view-only” access to certain parts). This prevents accidental or unauthorized changes – for instance, only a small group might be allowed to mark content as “Approved for air.” Also consider multi-channel or multi-department scenarios: one user might be a scheduler for Channel A but should not affect Channel B. The software should support scoping permissions per channel or department as needed. In practice, having tiered access protects content and schedule integrity ([MAM: Organize and Manage Media Content](https://www.orangelogic.com/media-asset-management-mam-in-digital-asset-management#:~:text=to%20meet%20the%20requirements%20of,various%20platforms%20and%20devices)). As a best practice, audit logs (mentioned later) combined with strong permission control ensure accountability and security in the system."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-32",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Frequent Scheduling Changes:** Identify which types of schedule changes are most common so the UI can make those quick and easy. Broadcasters often need to make **last-minute swaps** – for example, if a scheduled program is not ready or a new timely piece becomes available. The system should allow quick replacement of one program with another in the lineup (drag-and-drop or right-click swap functionality). Time changes are also frequent (e.g. shifting a start time by a few minutes), so editing a schedule entry’s time should be straightforward, with the system automatically adjusting subsequent items if needed. Another common change is **inserting filler content** when something runs short. Users might need one-click access to a library of short clips (PSAs, station IDs, bumpers) to fill a gap of a few minutes. Conversely, if something runs long, the user might need to drop the next scheduled item – the system could mark it as “preempted” and possibly alert the scheduler to reschedule it later. **Emergency alerts or live inserts** are special cases (see above), but the ability to quickly insert a live feed or important message is crucial. Also, changes in series episodes happen: e.g., if episode 5 isn’t delivered on time, they might re-run episode 4 in that slot – the scheduler should be able to find and place that content swiftly. In summary, the interface should be optimized for **speed** in making changes: perhaps a real-time view of what’s playing now with an option to “Take content off air” and replace it, or a shortcut to jump to “today’s schedule” for on-the-fly edits. Given that last-minute changes propagate to many outputs (on-air, online schedule, etc.), the system needs to update all connected systems in real time ([Broadcast Management System and TV Scheduling Software | VSN](https://www.vsn-tv.com/en/products/vsncrea/#:~:text=Maximum%20synchronization)) so that everyone (and every system) is on the same page."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-33",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Tracking Producer Information:** Maintain a directory of content producers and their associated content. For community channels (Cablecast’s typical user base), knowing who created each program is important for both credit and contact. The system should allow linking one or multiple **Producers** (people or organizations) to each content item. Store contact info, organization, and any notes (e.g. “Requires approval from City Council before each release” or “Has signed release forms on file”). This way, staff can quickly retrieve a producer’s information if there's an issue with a show (for example, to notify them of a schedule change or ask for a missing caption file). It’s also useful to see all content by a given producer – e.g. filter the library by producer name to find all their episodes. **Relationships** might also refer to collaborations or series ownership; e.g., a series might have an executive producer plus various episode producers. Capture these roles in metadata if needed. Best practice is to maintain a **producer profile** in the system: including their shows, past contributions, and any agreements (like a content license or an expiration date for their program). This helps in building a community as well – if a producer consistently provides popular content, the station may want to prioritize their new submissions. Additionally, tracking producers can feed into reports (how many active producers, how much airtime each producer received, etc., which is often a metric community media centers report to funding sources)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-34",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Common Pain Points (Current Software):** When designing the system, address known frustrations that users have with existing scheduling software. Some common pain points include:"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-36",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- *Clunky User Interface:* Older systems might require many clicks or have confusing layouts for building a schedule. Users want drag-and-drop simplicity and a clear timeline view. Ensure the new system is modern and intuitive to reduce training time."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-37",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- *Excessive Manual Work:* In some systems, tasks are not automated – e.g. retyping the same program info for each weekly airing, or manually calculating time fill. Eliminate redundancy by letting users **“create once, use many”** – for example, input program data once and reuse it, auto-fill recurring slots, and auto-adjust for program durations. Tight integration (write-once, publish-everywhere) is a selling point for modern systems ([Video Playback Servers, Automation, and Scheduling Software](https://www.cablecast.tv/solutions/automation#:~:text=WRITE%20ONCE%2C%20PUBLISH%20EVERYWHERE))."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-38",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- *Lack of Real-Time Updates:* A big frustration is when the scheduling system doesn’t communicate changes instantly, leading to different departments working with outdated info. By having a web-based, real-time system, all users see the latest schedule as soon as a change is made ([Broadcast Management System and TV Scheduling Software | VSN](https://www.vsn-tv.com/en/products/vsncrea/#:~:text=Maximum%20synchronization)). This avoids the “outdated spreadsheet” problem and the **paperwork** that comes with printing and distributing updated schedules ([Infinity Media Broadcast Solutions–Revolutionize Your Company](https://mdgdevelopers.com/overcoming-broadcast-industry-challenges-with-infinity-medias-innovative-real-time-programming-and-commercial-scheduling-solutions/#:~:text=The%20broadcast%20industry%20is%20faced,difficulties%20that%20plague%20the%20sector))."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-39",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- *Poor Conflict Resolution:* Users often complain that it’s hard to spot or fix conflicts in older software. The new system should visibly highlight overlaps or inconsistencies and offer suggestions (like which program could be moved to resolve it)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-40",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- *Limited Search/Filter:* If finding content is slow, it wastes time. As noted, robust search by metadata is key – this is a pain point when not available."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-41",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- *Insufficient Metadata Fields:* In some software, users cannot input all the info they want (for example, no field for content ratings or episode number). By allowing customizable or extensive metadata, you alleviate this issue ([Broadcast Management System and TV Scheduling Software | VSN](https://www.vsn-tv.com/en/products/vsncrea/#:~:text=match%20at%20L129%20VSNCrea%20can,at%20the%20choice%20of%20the))."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-42",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- *No Integration/Duplication of Effort:* A major challenge is having separate systems for linear broadcast, VOD, and streaming, which means entering schedules multiple times. Users want a unified workflow that **avoids duplicate data entry**, integrating all platforms ([Infinity Media Broadcast Solutions–Revolutionize Your Company](https://mdgdevelopers.com/overcoming-broadcast-industry-challenges-with-infinity-medias-innovative-real-time-programming-and-commercial-scheduling-solutions/#:~:text=The%20broadcast%20industry%20is%20faced,difficulties%20that%20plague%20the%20sector)). For instance, scheduling a program on cable should also allow one-click publishing to the web VOD schedule if needed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-43",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- *Reliability and Support:* Any crashes or instability in scheduling software is extremely painful, as it can jeopardize on-air accuracy. Ensure the system is thoroughly tested and provide support, as reliability will be a top requirement from a user perspective."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-44",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "By directly addressing these pain points (many of which revolve around inefficiency, outdated processes, and lack of integration ([Infinity Media Broadcast Solutions–Revolutionize Your Company](https://mdgdevelopers.com/overcoming-broadcast-industry-challenges-with-infinity-medias-innovative-real-time-programming-and-commercial-scheduling-solutions/#:~:text=The%20broadcast%20industry%20is%20faced,difficulties%20that%20plague%20the%20sector))), the broadcast management system will greatly improve user satisfaction and productivity."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-55",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-45",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Series vs. One-Time Broadcasts:** Implement best practices for handling recurring series differently from one-off programs. The system should have a concept of a **“Series” container** with individual episodes inside. This allows common metadata (series title, general description, genre) to be stored once at the series level and inherited by episodes, with episode-specific info (title, synopsis, number) stored per episode. This structure makes scheduling easier – a scheduler could, for example, drag a series onto the calendar and have the system prompt for which episode to fill, or even auto-place the next unpublished episode. It also helps in searching and organizing content: e.g. you can search the series name and see all episodes. **Series metadata** should include a unique series identifier, series title, and perhaps season numbers if applicable ([Series Metadata](https://docs.frequency.com/en/metadata-specifications/series-metadata.html#:~:text=series_guid)) ([Series Metadata](https://docs.frequency.com/en/metadata-specifications/series-metadata.html#:~:text=series_name)). Each episode is linked to that series via the ID. For **one-time broadcasts** (like a single special or a live event that won’t repeat), those can be handled as standalone content entries not tied to a series. Mark them accordingly so they’re not mistakenly treated as episodic content. Planning schedules can benefit from series management features: for instance, if a series airs weekly, the system can automatically slot new episodes in the weekly slot, or flag if you run out of episodes. Additionally, have the ability to schedule **“marathons” or reruns** easily by selecting multiple episodes of a series and dragging them into a schedule block. *Best practice:* use the series/episode model for anything episodic, even things like city council meetings (where each meeting is like an episode of the “City Council Meetings” series) – this way, recurring programs are neatly grouped. The series approach also helps populate EPGs and on-demand listings with richer information, since the series structure is recognized in many systems ([Series Metadata](https://docs.frequency.com/en/metadata-specifications/series-metadata.html#:~:text=Individual%20videos%20may%20now%20be,is%20not%20required%20for%20ingestion))."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-46",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Essential Metadata for Content Types:** Different types of content may need specific metadata, and the system should accommodate that. At minimum, every asset (series or one-time) should have: **Title, Description, Runtime, Format/Resolution, Audio info, Language, Original Air Date or Production Date, Content Rating (e.g. TV-G, PG…), and Rights/Expiration info**. For series episodes, add **Episode Title, Episode Number, Season Number (if used)** ([Series Metadata](https://docs.frequency.com/en/metadata-specifications/series-metadata.html#:~:text=season_number)) ([Series Metadata](https://docs.frequency.com/en/metadata-specifications/series-metadata.html#:~:text=Special%20Season)). For movies or films, include **year of release, actors or director (if relevant to your context), and possibly a reference ID (like an EIDR or IMDB ID)**. For community event videos, include **event date/location** in metadata. **Categorization** fields (genre, subgenre, tags) are important across all types to help filtering. Technical metadata (codec, file size, bitrate) can be recorded automatically on ingest, which helps the system plan playout (for example, knowing if a file is HD or SD for the appropriate channel). **Rights metadata** is critical: fields for allowed play count or dates (see below) and any restrictions (e.g. “must not air before XYZ date” or “web streaming not allowed”). **Credits information** (producer, host, guests) can also be part of metadata if needed for publication or internal reference. It’s a best practice to adopt a standard metadata schema or at least reference one for guidance – e.g. the **Dublin Core** or **EBU Core** for basic fields, and broadcast-specific extensions for things like parental rating and aspect ratio ([Standards: Part 26 - An Introduction To Metadata - The Broadcast Bridge - Connecting IT to Broadcast](https://www.thebroadcastbridge.com/content/entry/20984/standards-part-26-an-introduction-to-metadata#:~:text=There%20are%20few%20metadata%20standards,sourced)). The system should allow metadata editing and updates easily, and even customization – for instance, some broadcasters might add a field “Council District” for a government meeting video. VSN’s system allows custom metadata fields so that the stored info matches the broadcaster’s needs ([Broadcast Management System and TV Scheduling Software | VSN](https://www.vsn-tv.com/en/products/vsncrea/#:~:text=VSNCrea%20can%20be%20easily%20customized,the%20choice%20of%20the%20customer)). All metadata should be searchable, as noted, and also used when publishing content to other platforms (like automatically generating an EPG entry or populating a VOD description field from these metadata fields)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-47",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Content Version Tracking:** It’s important to handle multiple versions or revisions of a content item. A show might be edited after initial airing (to fix an error or shorten it), or there might be different versions for different platforms (with/without ads, etc.). The content management should incorporate **version control** for media assets ([MAM: Organize and Manage Media Content](https://www.orangelogic.com/media-asset-management-mam-in-digital-asset-management#:~:text=solutions)). One approach is to treat each version as a separate asset file but link them under the same content record. For example, “Program X – Episode 5” could have Version 1 (original 58:00 length) and Version 2 (edited 56:30 length with an extra disclaimer). The system should allow marking one as the “current” version for scheduling, but still keep the old one for archive or reference. All versions would share the same metadata except where differences apply (maybe air dates or small description differences). By tracking versions, you maintain an **audit of changes** to content – useful if questions arise about what exactly aired. It also prevents confusion; users can see at a glance that there’s a newer cut of a show. If your users often do re-edits, consider implementing check-in/check-out workflows or at least a clear version naming convention (e.g. Program ID + version number). Make sure any **replacement of a media file** in the library preserves the link in the schedule. For instance, if a file has audio issues and you replace it with a fixed file, the schedule referencing that content ID should play the updated file without requiring manual re-scheduling. Emphasize storing previous versions for compliance – e.g., if someone needs to verify what was aired last month, you have that exact file. A robust MAM (Media Asset Management) component with version control features is ideal: it will **track the evolution of media assets over time, ensuring the latest is used but old ones remain accessible if needed ([MAM: Organize and Manage Media Content](https://www.orangelogic.com/media-asset-management-mam-in-digital-asset-management#:~:text=solutions))**."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-48",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Preview and QC Capabilities:** Users should be able to preview content directly within the management system before it’s broadcast. This means having a built-in media player (or integration with one) to play back video files (using proxy files if necessary for performance). The **Content Catalog** should allow a user to click on an item and watch it, to verify content, check its quality, or just familiarize themselves with it ([Broadcast Management System and TV Scheduling Software | VSN](https://www.vsn-tv.com/en/products/vsncrea/#:~:text=Organize%20all%20your%20content%20,directly%20from%20VSNCrea%20as%20well)). Ideally, the preview includes video, audio, and even caption data if present (so one can check subtitles). Additionally, showing technical information alongside the preview (like runtime, resolution, and a visual timeline with markers for any segmentation or breaks) is helpful for QC. Some systems allow setting “chapter” markers or ad break points during preview – if relevant, integrate that so that metadata like segmentation can be added while previewing ([Broadcast Management System and TV Scheduling Software | VSN](https://www.vsn-tv.com/en/products/vsncrea/#:~:text=Organize%20all%20your%20content%20,directly%20from%20VSNCrea%20as%20well)). For multi-segment programs (say a city council meeting that has two parts with a break), the preview tool could let the content manager confirm the segments and their lengths. Having quick preview is also useful for schedulers to double-check they have the right program (e.g., if two episodes have similar names, they can play a few seconds to be sure). Ensure that previewing doesn’t risk the high-resolution original if bandwidth is a concern – often systems generate a lower-resolution **proxy file** for browsing and preview. The date and time of the file’s recording might be shown to catch if the wrong file was attached. Essentially, **don’t schedule blind** – always allow users to see and hear the content beforehand. This cuts down errors and builds confidence that when the schedule says Program X, it’s indeed the correct video."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-49",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Managing Content Rights & Expiration:** Keep strict track of content rights, such as how long you are allowed to air a program and under what conditions. Metadata fields should include **license start date** (earliest play date), **license end date** (after which content must not air), and possibly a maximum number of plays if that’s part of contracts. The system can then enforce these: e.g. warn or prevent a scheduler from placing a show outside its rights window or after expiration. For acquired content (like syndicated programs or movies), include fields for contract ID and distributor, plus any usage restrictions (e.g. “no streaming rights” or “only 4 plays total”). The schedule should automatically flag violations – for instance, if you try to schedule a film past its end date, it should alert you. It’s also helpful if the system can countdown plays or days remaining and notify content managers as expiration approaches (so they either renew rights or remove the content). Once a program expires, the system can mark it as **inactive/unavailable** for scheduling. In addition, manage **geographical rights** if applicable (some content might only be cleared for certain regions or platforms). In community media, content is often owned by the producer indefinitely, but there may be cases like a special event rights for a season. So this may be more crucial for larger cable networks with licensed content. Still, even community stations might impose an internal expiration to keep content fresh (e.g. only air PSAs for 6 months). The system should allow setting those policies. Furthermore, store any **release forms or rights documents** (perhaps as attachments) with the content record, so staff can easily verify that airing a show meets legal requirements. Managing rights also means respecting them in multi-platform distribution: if a show doesn’t have web rights, the system should not (or should warn not to) publish it to the web/VOD. All these measures ensure compliance and avoid legal issues with content providers. As VSN’s platform notes, centralizing metadata with broadcasting rights and contracts helps **avoid human error in scheduling** content outside allowed parameters ([Broadcast Management System and TV Scheduling Software | VSN](https://www.vsn-tv.com/en/products/vsncrea/#:~:text=Content%20acquisitions)). Finally, ensure that when rights expire, it’s clear if content should be deleted or archived, and possibly automate moving expired content out of the active library to an archive (so it’s not accidentally scheduled)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-50",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Content Regulations (Watershed Hours):** Be aware of regulations regarding when certain content can be shown. In many jurisdictions, “watershed” or safe harbor rules prohibit indecent or adult content during times when children are likely watching. For example, in the U.S., **broadcast** TV cannot air indecent content between 6am and 10pm ([Obscene, Indecent and Profane Broadcasts | Federal Communications Commission](https://www.fcc.gov/consumers/guides/obscene-indecent-and-profane-broadcasts#:~:text=Broadcasting%20obscene%20content%20is%20prohibited,may%20be%20in%20the%20audience)) (though cable-only channels are not legally bound by FCC indecency rules, many cablecasters self-regulate). The system should allow tagging content with a **rating or sensitivity level** (e.g. explicit language, violence level, etc.) and possibly enforce rules: e.g. prevent scheduling content rated TV-MA or flagged “adult” before 10:00 PM, or at least pop a warning. In the UK, a watershed at 9pm is standard for adult material. The scheduling software could have configurable policies per region or channel – for instance, you could set a rule that any program with a certain rating cannot be placed in a restricted time window. Also, maintain a field for the official **content rating** (like TV-PG, TV-14, etc. in the US system ([Television content rating system - Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Television_content_rating_system#:~:text=Television%20content%20rating%20systems%20are,of%20television%20programmes%20for%20minors))), and ensure that gets passed to the playout system to encode into the broadcast (for parental control devices). Having these in place shows due diligence in protecting minors. Additionally, **children’s programming regulations** (like limits on advertising during kids’ shows, or educational content requirements) might affect scheduling, though those are more content selection issues than technical scheduling – but you could produce reports to show you aired the required educational content hours, if applicable. Overall, incorporate any regional broadcast standards into the scheduling rules engine so that the user is guided to comply by design."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-51",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Tracking & Enforcing Content Ratings:** As part of compliance, the system should support the assignment and use of content ratings (parental guidelines). For each program, store its rating (e.g. TV-G, TV-14, etc., or movie ratings G/PG/PG-13/R). This rating should be visible to schedulers and possibly printed on program logs or guides. **Enforce** the rating policies in two ways: (1) **Operationally**, as mentioned, by restricting schedule placement (for example, don’t allow a TV-MA show in a daytime slot). (2) **Technical output**, by ensuring the rating info is sent to the broadcasting equipment so that it can encode the ratings for viewer advisory systems (like the V-chip or on-screen icons). Some broadcast systems require the rating to be part of the metadata in the automation playlist so that it can insert the appropriate signal. The scheduling system should allow setting up those ratings easily, maybe even auto-suggesting a rating based on the time or content keywords (though final verification is needed by a human). Keep a log of what rating each aired program had, in case of audience complaints or audits. In essence, treat ratings as mandatory metadata for content intended for public broadcast. Methods to enforce might include a simple popup: “You are scheduling a TV-MA program at 4pm, which is before watershed – are you sure?” or a hard block unless an admin override is given. This provides a safety net. Another aspect is **adherence to TV content regulations**: some countries require showing a rating advisory at the start of the program. The system could support this by scheduling a ratings slide or icon automatically based on the content’s rating metadata. While not strictly part of scheduling, it’s a nice integration for compliance. Overall, robust rating tracking protects the station legally and helps inform viewers properly."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-52",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Audit Trails:** Maintain a detailed audit log of user actions and schedule changes for compliance and accountability. This means every creation, modification, or deletion of a schedule entry or content record should be recorded with timestamp and user ID. In the event of a dispute (e.g. “Who approved this explicit show to air at 8pm?”), you can consult the audit trail to find out the chain of events. The audit log should ideally be tamper-evident (users, even admins, shouldn’t be able to alter the history). For compliance, regulators or internal policies might require retention of logs for a certain period. For instance, keep at least 60-90 days of detailed logs accessible, and archive older logs. Key things to log: user logins, content metadata edits, approval actions, scheduling actions (add/move/remove), and any manual override on the playout. If the system triggers external events (like sending a trigger to an EAS encoder or switching a feed), log that too. Many compliance standards (like SOC or ISO certifications) favor strong auditing of systems ([Best Practices for Authorization Audit Logs - Permit.io](https://www.permit.io/blog/audit-logs#:~:text=Compliance%20standards%2C%20such%20as%20HIPPA%2C,access%20controls%20and%20auditing%20mechanisms)), and while those might not be required for a cable scheduling system, it’s good practice. In addition to user action logs, **broadcast logs** (as-run logs mentioned earlier) are part of compliance: storing what actually aired and when. These serve as an audit trail of the content output. Solutions like Actus provide compliance logging by recording off-air feeds and extracting metadata ([Broadcast compliance logging and monitoring system for TV, Radio and Internet channels.](https://actusdigital.com/broadcast-compliance/#:~:text=broadcasters%20in%20order%20to%20comply,guarantees%20%C2%A0broadcasters%20ensure%20they%20are)) – your system might integrate with such a logger, or at least ensure that scheduled vs. actual discrepancies are noted. Overall, an audit trail feature can be as simple as an internal database table of events or a more visible “history” feature on each record (e.g. viewing a content item’s history shows when it was created and any edits). This not only helps with compliance but also with operational troubleshooting (like tracking down why a certain change happened)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-53",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Emergency Broadcast Handling:** Comply with emergency alert requirements by integrating the Emergency Alert System (EAS) into the workflow. In the US, all cable TV systems and broadcasters are required to participate in EAS, which means they must be able to **interrupt programming with emergency messages** from authorities ([Emergency Alert System | FEMA.gov](https://www.fema.gov/emergency-managers/practitioners/integrated-public-alert-warning-system/public/emergency-alert-system#:~:text=The%20Emergency%20Alert%20System%20,minutes%20during%20a%20national%20emergency)). Typically, an EAS unit (decoder/encoder) is connected to the broadcast chain; when an alert is received (national, state or local), it can override the channel feed. Your management system should support this in a few ways. First, ensure there is a mechanism to **immediately yield control** to the EAS signal. This might simply mean that the automation/playout system honors EAS break signals (often via relay closures or SNMP triggers). The scheduling system could show an “EAS ACTIVE” status when an alert is in progress and mark that in the as-run log. Second, after an EAS alert, regular programming should resume. The system might need rules for what to do after an interruption: e.g. resume where left off vs. jump to current time schedule. It’s common that the alert will cut in, then once done, programming rejoins in progress (since EAS often lasts a couple of minutes at most) ([Emergency Alert System | FEMA.gov](https://www.fema.gov/emergency-managers/practitioners/integrated-public-alert-warning-system/public/emergency-alert-system#:~:text=Messages%20can%20interrupt%20radio%20and,to%20broadcast%20emergency%20alert%20information)). Ensure your logs note that a certain program was interrupted by an emergency message at a certain time (for compliance and information to staff). Additionally, **weekly and monthly EAS tests** are required by FCC. These can be scheduled like any other content (often a short announcement or the EAS tones themselves). The system should facilitate scheduling these required tests (usually weekly RWTs and monthly RMTs) – possibly with a template or reminder so they aren’t missed. Also, consider other emergency scenarios: e.g. if there’s a need to quickly go live for a local emergency press conference, the system should allow an operator to break into the schedule and switch to a live feed. This is more an operational aspect, but the scheduling software can support it with a one-click “Go Live Now” function that essentially does an unscheduled manual event. Compliance-wise, ensure that such actions are logged and that any required reports (some jurisdictions require a report after an EAS activation) can be supported by the system data. In summary, emergency handling is both a technical integration and a set of procedures – the system should accommodate both so that legal obligations are met and important information reaches viewers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b-54",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Accessibility Requirements:** Incorporate features that ensure the broadcast meets accessibility laws and guidelines. **Closed captioning** is typically the top requirement – FCC rules mandate most television programming to be closed-captioned for the hearing impaired ([Closed Captioning on Television | Federal Communications Commission](https://www.fcc.gov/consumers/guides/closed-captioning-television#:~:text=Closed%20captioning%20displays%20the%20audio,close%20caption%20their%20TV%20programs)). Your system should track whether a given program has captions available (and possibly store caption files or links). When scheduling, it might flag content that is missing captions if it’s supposed to have them. Also, integrate with captioning workflows: e.g. if using an automated caption service or receiving caption files, ensure those are associated with the content in the system. Cablecast’s newer tools even include automated caption and translation services ([Video Playback Servers, Automation, and Scheduling Software](https://www.cablecast.tv/solutions/automation#:~:text=Closed%20Captioning%20%26%20Translation)), indicating the importance of this feature. For **audio description** (narration for the blind), larger broadcasters have quotas for certain hours of described content. If applicable, have a field or flag for “audio description available” and ensure those tracks get to the playout (perhaps via a secondary audio channel). At minimum, make sure the system doesn’t hinder providing secondary audio – it should allow scheduling of an alternate audio feed if needed. Also consider **accessible emergency information**: any text crawl for emergencies must be spoken aloud for visually impaired viewers (U.S. regulations require this on secondary audio). While this is handled at play-out, your scheduling system could help by indicating if a program has an emergency crawl (metadata) so the playout system can route the audio properly. Additionally, ensure any **consumer-facing outputs** (like a web schedule or on-screen guide) are accessible (e.g. web pages follow ADA web guidelines, with screen-reader-friendly design). Internally, the software UI should also be accessible to your staff (consider ARIA roles, high contrast, etc., so staff with disabilities can use the tool). Another legal consideration: **advertisement loudness (CALM Act)** – while this is more of an audio processing issue, you might log which spots aired to help demonstrate compliance if loudness is questioned. In short, **compliance with accessibility** means tracking and supporting closed captions, descriptive audio, and making sure no matter the viewer’s needs, the content can be received. Providing captioning is not just a legal checkbox but a public service – the system should help ensure 100% of required programming is captioned (with quality standards) by letting managers see caption status at a glance and generate needed documents (like certification of compliance, if asked by FCC or local authorities). By building these considerations into the content management and scheduling process, Cablecast operators can confidently meet legal accessibility obligations while serving their whole community."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}